## Three-Stone Tapered Baguette — The Classic Pick

The [Three-Stone Tapered Baguette in 14K White Gold (item 203640)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/three-stone-tapered-baguette-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-4-ct-tw-item-203640?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) carries 1/4 ct tw of tapered baguette diamonds — one on each side of the center stone, each tapering from wide at the center to narrow at the shank.

[![Three-stone tapered baguette diamond engagement ring in 14k white gold — Blue Nile item 203640](/images/radiant-cut-three-stone-tapered-baguette-ring-white-gold.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/three-stone-tapered-baguette-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-4-ct-tw-item-203640?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

The setting price is [$3,410](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/three-stone-tapered-baguette-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-4-ct-tw-item-203640?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). With the [1ct E-VVS1 IGI lab stone at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), total ring cost is $4,277 lab. With the [1ct G-VS2 GIA natural at $2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), total is $5,728 natural.

This is the setting that fully activates The Extension Effect. The wide end of each baguette sits directly against the corner of the radiant, with minimal visible gap. The taper moves outward and the eye follows it to the end of the finger. It's a proportioned, intentional design that ages well.

The 14K white gold basket sets the radiant with four corner prongs and two side bars that secure the baguettes. The shank narrows from the baguette junction onward. This ring reads as elegant in every casual context and as formal in every ceremony photo.

At 1/4 ct tw, the side stones are substantial enough to register visually but not so large they compete with the center stone. The radiant still reads as the clear focal point. That balance is harder to achieve than it sounds — many baguette settings either set side stones too small to matter or so large they overwhelm the center.

